{"content":"💡 **SUGGESTION: the crossing predicate is now duplicated — extract a shared helper**

`_headlessSteerDue` re-implements the exact bucket-crossing check `_steerHeadlessLiveness` applies ~35 lines below:

```dart
// here (new):
if (!job.isRunning || !job.notifyOnSettle) continue;
final elapsed = _waitingClock().difference(job.startedAt);
final elapsedMs = elapsed.isNegative ? 0 : elapsed.inMilliseconds;
if (elapsedMs ~/ liveness.quietMs > (liveness.consumedBucket[job.id] ?? 0)) return true;

// and again in _steerHeadlessLiveness:
if (currentBucket <= lastBucket) continue; // no new crossing
```

The doc comment states this is "the same crossing check [_steerHeadlessLiveness] applies" — but nothing enforces that. The steer already evolved once (the multi-bucket burst handling), and a future change to either side — e.g. a different bucketing rule or a new suppression condition — would silently diverge the sleep guard from the steer, re-opening exactly the spin window this PR closes. A shared private predicate (e.g. `_headlessCrossedBucket(job, liveness)` returning the current bucket or -1) used by both would make the "same check" claim structural instead of textual.
","path":"outputs/pr_review_comments/comment2.md"}

{"content":"💡 **SUGGESTION: use the captured `now` instead of re-reading the clock inside `_headlessSteerDue`**

This round now reads the waiting clock twice on two different code paths:

- `now` (line 245) feeds both `liveness.wakeIn(...)` and `deadline.difference(now)`;
- `_headlessSteerDue` reads `_waitingClock()` again internally for its elapsed computation.

So `wake`, `steerDue`, and `remainingCeiling` are computed against two potentially different time bases. Under the fake-clock test seam both reads usually coincide (the fake clock advances only on sleeps), and in production the skew is microseconds — but at an exact threshold boundary the two reads can disagree on whether a crossing is due, i.e. `wakeIn` can see `wake <= 0` from a threshold while `_headlessSteerDue` sees it not-yet-due (or vice versa), producing one avoidable instant round. Passing `now` into `_headlessSteerDue` (one extra parameter) keeps the round on a single time base and makes the predicate trivially pure.
","path":"outputs/pr_review_comments/comment3.md"}

